Don't call methods directly.  Although you fail to give credit,
glm.nb is Bill Venables' function from library MASS (not mass).
You don't give a reproducible example (despite your claim), but for the
example on the glm.nb help page the results *are* on the correct scale,
as indeed they are for my emulation of the problem below.

You don't tell us the version of MASS or of S-PLUS, but I can only surmise
that you have a (very) outdated version, or that you have masked some
system functions (as you have used the names of at least two in the
non-functional code you give).

> The following S+ code reproduces the code.

It doesn't: there is no data set called oslo in MASS or S-PLUS.

> > library(mass)
> > poisson <- glm(count ~ time, data = oslo, family = poisson)
> > negbin <- glm.nb(count ~ time, data = oslo)
> > coef(poisson)
>  (Intercept)       time
>    0.1406699 0.2401174
> > coef(negbin)
>  (Intercept)       time
>    0.1406698 0.2401175
> > poisson.pred <- expand.grid(time = c(1, 2))
> > poisson.pred <- cbind(poisson.pred, response = predict.glm(poisson,
> predict, type = "response"))

What is predict supposed to be here?  It's a system function.

> > print(poisson.pred)
>      time response
> 1   1      1.151045
> 2   2      1.463436
>
> ## These values are too high:#########
> > negbin.pred <- expand.grid(time = c(1, 2))
> > negbin.pred <- cbind(negbin.pred, response = predict.glm(negbin, predict,
> type =        "response"))
> > print(predict)
>      time response
> 1   1      3.161493
> 2   2     4.320781
>
> ## These values are OK: #########
> > negbin.pred <- expand.grid(time = c(1,2))
> > negbin.pred <- cbind(negbin.pred, response = predict.glm(negbin, predict,
> type =        "link"))
>      time response
> 1   1      1.151045
> 2   2      1.463436

Note:

> exp(c(1.151045, 1.463436))
[1] 3.161495 4.320780

This does not happen in my experiments.

library(MASS)
oslo <- data.frame(count=rpois(10, 0.14+0.24*(1:10)), time = 1:10)
fit1 <- glm(count ~ time, data = oslo, family = poisson)
fit2 <- glm.nb(count ~ time, data = oslo)
newdata <- expand.grid(time = c(1,2))

predict(fit1, newdata, type="response")
        1         2
 0.542434 0.6578598

predict(fit2, newdata, type="response")
        1         2
 0.5421103 0.6575453

Something you have done has broken your installation, I believe.

