> help("storage.mode")

When mode(x) is "numeric", the data of
       x may be stored as integers,  or  as  single-precision  or
       double-precision  floating  point numbers: storage.mode(x)
       will be "integer", "single" or "double" correspondingly.

Looks like that what we get depends on **how the result is stored**. See
storage.mode for the result d in the example below. If the result is
stored as integer then there is an overflow, which manifests as NA or
incorrect result (1470457856). If the result is stored as double-precision
everything is fine.

Note that the way result is stored is quite sensitive to the way
expression is written. For instance we can change the way the result is
stored by changing 560 to 560.0 (see below) or by using 500^2 instead of
500*500.

S-PLUS : Copyright (c) 1988, 1998 MathSoft, Inc.
S : Copyright Lucent Technologies, Inc.
Version 5.0 Release 3 for Sun SPARC, SunOS 5.5 : 1998

> x <- 560
> y <- 440
> z <- 500
> w <- 500
> d <- x*y*z*w

> storage.mode(d)
[1] "integer"
> d
[1] NA

> x <- 560.0
> d <- x*y*z*w
> storage.mode(d)
[1] "double"
> d
[1] 6.16e+10

> d <- 560*440*(500^2)
> storage.mode(d)
[1] "double"
> d
[1] 6.16e+10

