From bkb5 at cdc.gov  Wed Jun 20 21:08:47 2001
From: bkb5 at cdc.gov (Biggerstaff, Brad J.)
Date: Wed, 20 Jun 2001 17:08:47 -0400
Subject: The function "?".
Message-ID: <476F432195CDD111B18200008326BA50010949A5@mcdc-ftc-1.cdc.gov>

I recall a few years back someone posted an ammendment to "?" to return the
arguments of a function using the call 

>??fun

for the function "fun".

I couldn't find it in the archives, so I wrote another version.  I also
wrote it so that ??x returns the names of the object x when x is a list (so
data frame) object.  Of course, ?fun and ?x behave as before.  Other
variations are explained in the notes in the beginning lines of the
function, which I include below.  The new lines (there're only 5 or 6) are
noted.  

Now, I know it's not good form to rewrite built-in S-Plus functions with the
same name, but this seems to me an acceptable exception.  Of course, for
this to work, this version of "?" should be in a directory in the path above
C:\\SP2000\\splus\\_Functio or the equivalent in your S-Plus version.  This
was written and so far has worked on S-Plus 2000 Professional for Windows
(NT and 2000).

I welcome comments and suggestions for improvement.

Regards
Brad

Brad Biggerstaff, Ph.D.
Mathematical Statistician
Division of Vector-Borne Infectious Diseases
National Center for Infectious Diseases
Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
P.O. Box 2087
Fort Collins, CO  80522-2087
(970) 221-6473

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-------------------------------
"?"<-
function(e1, e2)
{
# This function is an edited version of the S-Plus function "?"
# which permits the call "??" to provide a shortcut to the function
# args() for functions and to names() for list objects.  
# The following illustrate the use:
# 1. Provide the help page for "?":
#    either
#    > ?
#    or
#    > ??
#    will work.
#
# 2. Get the help page for the function f:
#    > ?f
#  
# 3. Get the arguments for fucntion f:
#    > ??f
#    which is equivalent to args(f)
#
# 4. Get the options for the call f(x):
# > ?f(x)
#    which is the default behavior of "?".
#
# 5. By design, the call ??f(x) will still
#    return the arguments of f, as with ??f.
#    This was done because ?f(x) is already
#    taken care of, and one is presumed to
#    want the arguments when using two ?s.
#
# 6. The case ??x and x inherits from a list (so 
#    x can be a data frame) returns the names of x.
#
# 7. For other object classes, ??x returns an error.
#
#  Author:
#  Brad Biggerstaff -- bbiggerstaff at cdc.gov
#
#
        first.call.from <- function(n)
        {
                nn <- sys.parents()
                cc <- sys.calls()
                if(n == 1) nn[1] <- 0   # don't select frame 1
                nn <- seq(along = nn)[nn == n][1]
                if(is.na(nn))
                        NULL
                else if(is.name(cname <- cc[[nn]][[1]]))
                        list(frame = nn, name = as.character(cname))
                else NULL
        }
        n <- nargs()
        x1 <- substitute(e1)    #
#
# new for ??
        x1.mode <- as.character(mode(x1))
        x1 <- as.character(x1)
        if(x1[1] == "?") {
                if(is.list(get(x1[2]))) return(names(get(x1[2])))
                else return(args(unlist(parse(text = x1[2]))[1]))
        }
#
# that's all until below
        invisible(if(n == 0) {
                help("?")
        }
        else if(deparse((a1 <- substitute(e1))) == "." && n == 1) {
                n <- sys.parent()
                cc <- first.call.from(n)
                if(!length(cc))
                        stop("No context for ?")
                if(n == 1 && cc$name == "?")
                        help("?")
                else do.question.arg(cc$frame, cc$name)
        }
        else if(is.name(a1) || is.character(a1)) {
                is.a <- exists(a1)
                givewarn <- T
                res <- NULL     # default ret value, not clearly specified
by docs
                if((is.a && mode(get(a1)) == "function") ||
length(find.doc(a1)
                        )) {
                        givewarn <- F
                        help(as.character(a1))
                }
                if(is.a) {
                        e1 <- get(a1)
                        if(length(class(e1))) {
                                res <- do.question.object(e1)
                                if(attr(res, "possible")) {
                                  givewarn <- F
                                }
                                attributes(res) <- NULL
                        }
                }
                if(givewarn) {
                        cat("No documentation available for \"", a1,
"\",\n", 
                                "nor for the class of an object named \"",
a1, 
                                "\"\n", sep = "")
                }
                res
        }
        else if(is.call(a1) && length(a1) > 1) {
                arg1 <- a1[[2]]
                if(a1[[1]] == "methods")
                        do.question.methods(a1)
                else if(is.name(arg1) && exists(arg1))
                        do.question.call(a1, eval(arg1, sys.parent()))
                else help(as.character(a1[[1]]))
        }
        else {
#
# new for ??
                if(x1.mode == "?") return(do.call("?")) #
#
# that's all that's new
                cat("? only meaningful if followed by name or call\n", file
= 
                        "|stderr")
                stop()
        }
        )
}

