I've copied the listserv on my response because I am unable to
ground parts of my response in the literature and would appreciate
feedback from other readers on my positions.

> Hello,
> I have been performing cluster analysis with data for 20 to 30 individuals
> with approximately 40 attributes per individual.  I have been using the
> partitioning around medoids (pam) algorithm because 1) I can choose the
> number of clusters, 2) silhouette plots aid in selecting an optimal number
> of clusters and 3) the clusplot function for visualizing clusters.  I have
> 2 questions regarding pam and, I suppose, cluster analysis in general.

40 attributes sounds like a lot.  Given that you have at most n=30,
you cannot have a rank of 40.  In my experience, usually when I have
a large number of variables, the real number of underlying dimensions
is much smaller, i.e., if I do PCA there are many eigenvalues close
to 1.  I suggest finding an appropriate number (usually much
smaller) of latent variables and using them in the cluster analysis
rather than raw variables.  The risk in using raw variables is that
you implicitly weight up dimensions in the analysis that have more
variables.  For example, suppose you had 2 underlying dimensions;
you have 10 variables that are exact copies of the first dimension
and 1 of the second; the first dimension gets 10 times the weight
when evaluating the objective function.  Everitt's book on cluster
analysis gives some discussion of using principal components, but
doesn't discuss this hazard.  

> 1)When is it appropriate to standardize measurements (set stand=T)?  At
> one point I believed you should only standardize when attributes were in
> different units.  Someone suggested I standardize measurements when one
> particular attribute was consistently larger than other values (even
> though all units were of the same type) to try to minimize the influence
> of this one attribute.

With K-means, if the measuring units are not commensurate, you
should do something to make them commensureate.  Variables with
large variation implicitly get more weight in the analysis.  Everitt
(third edition, 1993, Cluster Analysis) gives a good example in
figures 5.1 and 5.2 for k-means.

> 2)After the algorithm chooses the clusters, is there a way to discover
> which attribute or attributes have the largest influence in determining
> clusters?  With a large number of attributes, the choice of clusters is
> oftentimes not obvious by just looking at the attributes of each
> individual in the cluster.

An ANOVA model quantifies within and between cluster variance.

